  "title": "Pentax ME-F",
  "subtitle": "The first SLR that focused its own lens — sensors in the body, a motor in the lens, and a red dot you dialed into alignment",
  "tags": ["Camera", "Autofocus", "Consumer", "Sensing", "Embedded Computing"],
  "blurb": "The Pentax ME-F (1981) was the first 35 mm single-lens reflex to autofocus — and the way it did it is the interface. Focus sensors lived in the camera *body*, but the motor lived in a special SMC Pentax AF 35–70 mm lens. You confirmed focus by turning the ring until a red dot lit in the viewfinder; the camera then drove the lens motor to match. It was a hybrid: human judgement as the reference, a motor as the actuator, a single LED as the whole conversation. The ancestor of every autofocus SLR that followed.",
